# Bloc state management

## Structure
- One Bloc per screen or per independent section. Home uses independent Blocs for the member header, banners, each product section and shops so one failing section never affects another.
- Files: presentation/bloc/<name>/<name>_bloc.dart with <name>_event.dart and <name>_state.dart as part files.
- Events are sealed classes named as past-tense facts. States are sealed or freezed; use ViewState<T> for read-only sections.
- Cubit is allowed only for trivial synchronous app-level state: ConnectivityCubit and LocaleCubit. Everything else is a Bloc with events.

## Concurrency
- Register every handler with a bloc_concurrency transformer.
- droppable for submits and anything a double tap could repeat.
- restartable for watch, refresh and search handlers so a new request cancels the previous one.
- sequential for ordered work such as quantity changes.

## Reactive reads
- Watch Drift streams through use cases with emit.forEach or emit.onEach. Map each Result to a ViewState with ViewState.fromResult.
- Emit loading first, then data, empty or failure. A retry event re-enters the same handler.

## Dependencies
- Blocs depend on use cases only. Never on repositories, DAOs, other Blocs, BuildContext or GetIt.
- The UI coordinates between Blocs when needed, through BlocListener.

## Providing and consuming
- Create BlocProviders at route level from get_it factories inside lib/config/routes builders. App-level Blocs (SessionBloc, ConnectivityCubit, LocaleCubit) are provided in lib/app.dart.
- Use BlocBuilder with buildWhen, BlocSelector for fine-grained rebuilds and BlocListener with listenWhen for one-shot effects.
- Model one-shot effects as distinct state transitions (for example an AddToCartStatus moving to added), never as flags that need resetting.

## Observability
- AppBlocObserver logs transitions and errors through AppLogger in debug and profile builds only.
- Every Bloc test uses bloc_test and covers loading, data, empty and failure paths.
